WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:01.920
Facebook announcing a new digital currency

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:04.640
and aims to make it as easy to send money around the world

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.260
as it is to send a photo.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:08.357
Facebook wants to befriend your wallet.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:10.960
We are fully committed to working with regulators

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.460
here and around the world.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:16.180
David Marcus is a Facebook executive who was called to testify

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.460
in two congressional hearings this week,

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:22.180
after releasing a white paper last month for a new digital currency.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:25.700
Satoshi Nakamoto is the pseudonym of a mysterious software developer

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:29.280
who released a paper describing the cryptocurrency Bitcoin.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.880
on an obscure mailing list in 2008,

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:34.340
and then disappeared two years later.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.840
So we are discussing a currency

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.580
controlled by an undemocratically selected coalition

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:42.860
of largely massive corporations.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:46.060
This is not Silicon Valley.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:49.100
Facebook now wants to control the money supply.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:52.500
Like a toddler who has gotten his hands on a book of matches

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.460
What could possibly go wrong?

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:57.740
Satoshi Nakamoto created a decentralized software network

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.120
that no government agency

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.940
has the authority to regulate or control.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:04.200
We've got the Treasury, the Federal Reserve,

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.120
the SEC, the FDIC, the OCC,

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.080
CFPB, the NCUA, the CFTC,

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:12.300
the FHFA.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.380
Which of all of these regulators

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:15.760
would have a piece

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:18.020
of creating the rules of the road?

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.260
Do you believe currency is a public good?

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:22.600
Congresswoman, I believe that sovereign currencies are sovereign.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.040
Do you believe Libra should be a public good?

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.280
Congresswoman again,

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.380
we will work with all of the regulators and

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.640
and address all concerns,

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.543
and the regulators will definitely...

00:01:33.543 --> 00:01:36.860
I'll take that as a no?

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:38.820
I should take that as a no?

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.440
It is not for me to decide Congresswoman.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.480
Okay, I'll take that as a no.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:46.060
In the end, Congress has a great deal of power.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:48.380
But one of the most important differences between

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.440
Facebook's Libra and Bitcoin

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:54.800
is that it can never make Satoshi Nakamoto sit in that chair.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.500
It takes a breathtaking amount of arrogance,

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:00.500
a breathtaking amount of arrogance to look at that track record

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.500
and think, you know what...

